\section*{Conclusion} 
In summary, we have studied the interaction of chiral electromagnetic modes with chiral matter within a macroscopic electrodynamics framework. To obtain chiral electromagnetic modes, we have developed a wideband handedness-preserving mirror based on one-dimensional periodic gratings placed on top of the Bragg mirror. Using a pair of these mirrors, we have formed a Fabry-Pérot resonator that supports chiral modes with eigenfrequencies dependent on the angle between the orientation of the upper and lower gratings. To model the chiral medium, we have used an approximation of the Pasteur medium, which is characterized by the local macroscopic chirality parameter $\kappa$. To properly describe the interaction of chiral modes with the chiral medium, we have introduced the Lorentz pole into the macroscopic parameters $\varepsilon(\omega)$, $\kappa(\omega)$, and $\mu(\omega)$ with oscillator strengths connected via the microscopic chirality parameter $\xi$. Then, we have placed the Pasteur medium into the Fabry-Pérot resonator between the mirrors. Using the formalism of Jones matrices, we have demonstrated that material resonance in the medium's macroscopic parameters induces strong coupling between chiral Fabry-Pérot modes and the chiral medium, with the Rabi splitting governed by the enantiomeric compatibility of the mode and the medium. Finally, we have confirmed our theoretical findings by comparing them with the results of full-wave simulations made by the Fourier modal method adapted for chiral media. 

We believe that our work provides a foundation for future research on chiral light-matter interactions, paving the way for innovative optical sensing of chiral organic molecules and enantiomer-selective technologies.


%enhancing the interaction between chiral light and chiral matter is essential for advancing optical detection technologies capable of distinguishing between enantiomers in chiral organic molecules. A promising approach involves designing resonators that support photonic modes with non-zero electromagnetic handedness, enabling selective interactions with molecules of opposite chirality. Conversely, placing chiral molecules in resonator hotspots can modify the system's properties due to their intrinsic electric and magnetic dipole moments. This study investigates these interactions by incorporating the Lorentz pole into the macroscopic parameters of the chiral medium --— namely, dielectric permittivity, magnetic permeability, and the chirality (Pasteur) parameter. The latter serves as a key macroscopic measure of chirality, coupling electric and magnetic fields in the constitutive relations. Our findings reveal that introducing the Lorentz pole into these parameters leads to chiral strong coupling between light and matter, with the coupling strength influenced by both the medium's chirality and the photonic mode's handedness.
%We propose a Fabry-Pérot resonator with anisotropic, handedness-preserving mirrors to sustain chiral modes, while a chiral medium ---  modelled using the chiral Lorentz approach --- is placed in the resonator gap. Our results demonstrate that material resonance in the medium's macroscopic parameters induces strong coupling between chiral photonic modes and the chiral medium, with the interaction strength governed by the enantiomeric compatibility of the mode and the medium.


