and consider the set $X=\mappingsp{T}{S}$ of all continuous maps from $T$ to $S$. 
Fix a point $t_0\in T$, and for each $r>0$, define 
\[
  \closedball{r}=\{t\in T\mid d_T(t, t_0)\leq r\}
\]
to be the closed ball centered at $t_0$ for each $r>0$. 
Then, for each $f, g\in X$, define 
\[
    d_r(f, g)=\sup_{x\in \closedball{r}} d_S(f(x),g(x)).
\] 

It is straightforward to verify that $(X, d_r)$ satisfies the axioms of \defref{definition axioms of dr}. 
Moreover, the topology induced by $\{d_r\}_{r>0}$ coincides with the compact-open topology on $\mappingsp{T}{S}$.
