\begin{abstract}
Small low-density exoplanets are sculpted by strong stellar irradiation but their origin is unknown. Two competing\citep{valencia_diversity_2025, Zeng2019} scenarios are that they formed either with rocky interiors and \ce{H2}-\ce{He} atmospheres (`gas-dwarfs';  e.g. \citet{Rogers2023,owen_evap_2017}), or instead with bulk compositions dominated by \ce{H2O} phases (`water-worlds'; e.g. \citet{luque_density_2022,Burn2024}). Observations of L 98-59 d have revealed its unusually low density and spectral characteristics \citep{banerjee_atmospheric_2024, gressier_hints_2024}, opening a window for disentangling its origin \citep{pass_cosmic_2025}. We constrain the possible range of evolutionary histories linking L 98-59 d's birth conditions to these observations, using a coupled atmosphere-interior modelling framework \citep{lichtenberg_vertically_2021, nicholls_convective_2025, nicholls_tidal_2025}. Observations are explained by a chemically-reducing mantle and early substantial ($>$1.8 mass\%) sulfur and hydrogen content, inconsistent with both the gas-dwarf and water-world origin scenarios. Observed spectral features are explained by in-situ photochemical production of \ce{SO2} in an \ce{H2} background \citep{tsai_photochemically_2023}. L 98-59 d's interior comprises a permanent magma ocean, allowing long-term retention of volatiles within its interior \citep{DornLichtenberg2021,shorttle_k218b_2024} over billions of years, consistent with surveyed Kepler Survey trends \citep{david_cks_2021}. Modelling of the `cosmic shoreline' suggests \citep{ji_shoreline_2025} that early volatile-metal budget is a determinant of atmospheric retention - here, we explicitly show that large initial budgets can explain JWST observations of some super-Earth planets (including DDT targets). In breaking with the supposed gas-dwarf/water-world dichotomy, we reveal an evolutionary pathway defined by high-molar-mass atmospheres overlying magma oceans, and invite a more nuanced taxonomy of small exoplanets.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{exoplanet, super-Earth, magma, redox, chemistry, atmosphere, jwst, escape, volatile, inventory}
