Formation of \ce{SO2} from \ce{H2S}, in the presence of \ce{OH} radicals from photolysis \ce{H2O}, can explain the detections of \ce{SO2}. This mechanism produces \ce{SO2} within the hot-Jupiter WASP-39b \cite{tsai_photochemically_2023, powell_sulfur_2024}. We infer the formation of \ce{SO2} by making a comparison between \textsc{VULCAN} photochemical kinetics models and the free-chemistry retrievals. Fig. \ref{fig:photochem} plots atmospheric mixing ratios for this case study under three chemical paradigms: \textsc{vulcan} SNCHO photochemical kinetics (solid lines), kinetics without photochemistry (dashed lines), and the equilibrium volatile outgassing applied during our evolutionary calculations (triangle markers at surface). The mixing ratio of \ce{SO2} (solid blue line) increases with altitude for $p<\SI{6}{\bar}$, while the equivalent case without photochemistry (dashed blue line) quenches at negligible abundance. Photochemical production of \ce{SO2} is necessary to raise its abundance to that consistent with the JWST observations (scatter points). Lime-coloured profiles show that \ce{H2S} abundance is consistent with the observational constraints under all three chemical paradigms; \ce{H2S} is thermochemically favoured to carry sulfur in the \ce{H2}-rich background. 

Detections of \ce{SO2} cannot be explained by volcanism at the surface \citep{nicholls_tidal_2025}, which would require \ce{SO2} to be transported into the observable upper atmosphere without being thermochemically reduced. Transport of \ce{SO2} (\SI{64}{\amu}) would have to take place in the absence of deep convection (Fig. \ref{fig:casestudy}b) and therefore only by diffusion through the \ce{H2} background. This physically unlikely scenario instead points to the formation of \ce{SO2} in-situ, requiring only the presence of otherwise poorly-constrained \ce{H2O}. This finding represents the first indication of active sulfur photochemistry occurring on a super-Earth exoplanet, and highlights the chemical interactions which link deep planetary interiors and their observable atmospheres.

