The absence of the anti-localization in the system with the defect for which
$K=K_0$ is clearly recognizable.

In Fig.~\ref{V_boundary_t1.pdf} the same numerical solution $v_0(t)$   for $\beta$-FPUT chain with
a mass-spring defect with $K=K_0$, which is already shown in Fig.~\ref{V_boundary_t.pdf}, is compared 
with analogous solutions with $K=0.9K_0$ and $K=1.1K_0$. We can see that the
solution corresponding to $K=0.9K_0$ vanishes faster than one taken for $K=K_0$. The corresponding order of decay
is expected to be $1/\sqrt{t^3}$, see Sect.~\ref{sect-isotope}. On the other hand, the
solution corresponding to $K=1.1K_0$ is a non-vanishing one, i.e., an impurity
induced localized oscillation is observed.

Thus, we have shown that the
anti-localization is not observed in both linear and nonlinear systems with a mass-spring defect if 
the system parameters correspond to 
the boundary of the localization domain in the problem parameter space. On
the other hand, the anti-localization is observed if the system parameters lie
outside this boundary.

