\end{figure} 

\begin{comment}
To understand the effect in the $t$-direction, we can assume $n=1$ and directly computes that for the Lorentzian inner product
\begin{equation*}
\langle P'(t), P'(t)\rangle= \frac{(1+a^2)}{(t^2-(1+a^2))^2}    
\end{equation*}
which has minimum (for $t\in[-a,a]$) exactly $\frac{1}{1+a^2}$ at $t=0$. Putting everything together, we see that $P^{-1}$ has Lipschitz constant $\sqrt{1+a^2}=\cosh(R)$.

\textit{Step 5. The Lipschitz constant of $\pi$. }We will denote points in $\mathbb{R}^4$ as $(z,\mathbf{x})$ with $\mathbf{x}=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$. Let $v_i=(z_i,\mathbf{p}_i)$ the vertices of $T$ (and therefore of $\Delta_{eucl}$). We begin by describing the map $\pi$. Given $(\cosh(R),\mathbf{q})\in \Delta_{hor}$, we need to determine $s=s(\mathbf{q})\in(0,1]$ so that
\begin{equation*}
