Several protocols are used to induce QT in a superfluid sample. They generally rely on continuous pumping of energy. A predominant approach involves sweeping a repulsive potential \cite{Kwon14,Navon16}. This procedure is often too violent, complicating the study of QT, as a result of the generation of a variety of excitations. 
As an alternative, one may wonder whether QT can emerge spontaneously, as topological defects emerge across a continuous phase transition. In this context, the Kibble-Zurek mechanism (KZM) constitutes a powerful paradigm for describing universal critical dynamics \cite{Kibble76a,Kibble76b,Zurek96a,Zurek96b,DZ14}. It does so by exploiting equilibrium properties such as the divergence of the correlation length $\xi=\xi_0/|\varepsilon|^{\nu}$ and the relaxation time $\tau=\tau_0/|\varepsilon|^{z\nu}$ in the neighborhood of the critical point \cite{DZ14}. Here, $\nu$ is the correlation length critical exponent,  $z$ is the dynamic critical exponent, and $\varepsilon=\lambda-\lambda_c$ for the control parameter $\lambda$ (e.g., temperature) with critical value $\lambda_c$. For a linearized quench $\varepsilon=t/\tau_Q$, the KZM introduces the universal scaling of the nonequilibrium correlation length $\hat\xi$ with the quench time $\tau_Q$, i.e., $\hat\xi=\xi_0(\tau_Q/\tau_0)^{\nu/(1+z\nu)}$. For point-like defects, such as solitons in a cigar-shaped BEC \cite{Damski_soliton_2010,Lamporesi13}, and vortices in a pancake BEC \cite{Weiler08}, the KZM further predicts the formation of vortices at a density that scales universally with the quench time $n\propto 1 / \hat{\xi}^{D}=\left( 1 / \xi_0^D \right) \left( \tau_0 / \tau_Q \right)^{D\nu/(1+z\nu)}$, where $D$ denotes the spatial dimension. In addition, it dictates that the response of the system is delayed by the freeze-out time scale $\hat{t}=(\tau_0 \tau_Q^{z\nu})^{1/(1+z\nu)}$. In scenarios characterized by $U(1)$ symmetry breaking that lead to vortex formation, the KZM has been widely tested in theoretical studies \cite{DRP11,Chesler15,Liu2020,Zeng23,Thudiyangal24}, with several experiments also supporting its role in superfluid formation and the BEC transition \cite{Weiler08,Navon15,Shin19,KimShin22}. 

In this Letter, we describe spontaneous quantum turbulence (SQT) in a newborn BEC prepared by a thermal quench in finite time. We establish the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy and demonstrate its universal scaling for different values of the quench time when rescaled by the nonequilibrium correlation length predicted by the Kibble-Zurek mechanism. 

{\it Kibble-Zurek dynamics of the BEC transition.} %--- 
To describe the non-equilibrium condensate dynamics and the emergence of QT, we consider the two-dimensional (2D) stochastic projected Gross-Pitaevskii equation \cite{Gardiner2003,blakie2008dynamics,Rooney2012,Rooney2014, Bradley2015, McDonald2020}. 
This formalism has been widely used to model the BEC transition, with numerous studies demonstrating its quantitative agreement with experimental observations \cite{Weiler08, Liu_kibble_2020}.
Specifically, it describes the evolution of the low-energy coherent modes of the system via a complex order parameter $\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}$, which evolves according to 
%
\begin{equation}\label{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE_main}
    d{\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}} = \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{C}} \left[ -(i + \gamma) \left( H_{sp} + g |\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}|^{2} -\mu\right) \Psi_{\mathcal{C}} \, dt + d \eta \right]. 
\end{equation}
Here, $H_{sp} = - \left( 1 / 2 \right) \nabla^{2} + V(\mathbf{r})$ denotes the single-particle Hamiltonian, where $V(\mathbf{r})$ is the external trapping potential. In this work, we focus on a periodic homogeneous system with $V(\mathbf{r})=0$. Further details of Eq.~(\ref{2D_dimensionless_SPGPE_main}) and its numerical implementation are provided in \cite{SM}.

To drive the phase transition, we consider a linear quench of the chemical potential, $\mu(t) = \mu_i + \left( \mu_f - \mu_i \right)t/\tau_Q$, with $\mu_i$ and $\mu_f$ denoting its initial and final values, respectively. This protocol drives the system through the critical point $\mu_c$ at a finite rate $\tau_Q$, resulting in the formation of a BEC populated with vortices. A vortex is characterized by a quantized circulation of the superfluid velocity,
$\oint \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{r},t)\cdot d \boldsymbol{\ell}= \left( 2\pi\hbar / m \right) w$, where $w\in\mathbb{Z}$ is the winding number.
The associated singularity of the velocity field enforces a vanishing condensate density at the vortex center,  the healing length determining the core size. 
%According to KZM, vortices spontaneously form in the course of the phase transition.
An example of a single realization of the newborn BEC is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling}, where vortices can be recognized by the depletion of the BEC density at their core. 
The associated phase map indicates that only the values $w=\pm1$ are generated in the course of the BEC transition. This is consistent with the fact that higher winding numbers are energetically costly and unstable against decay \cite{Tsubota08}.

Using the mean field values of the critical exponents $\nu = 1/2$ and $z = 2$, the KZM predicts the scaling of the freeze-out time, which sets the time scale in which the growth of the order parameter lags behind the crossing of the critical point set by the quench of $\mu(t)$. The equilibration time, 
when the growth of the number of bosons in BEC changes from exponential to linear \cite{Chesler15,Thudiyangal24,SM}, 
is proportional to the freeze-out time and scales universally as $t_{eq}\propto \hat{t}\propto\tau_Q^{1/2}$. This scaling is confirmed by the numerical results shown in the inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling} (c), where $\langle t_{eq} \rangle = \left( 3.843 \pm 0.460 \right) \tau_Q^{0.573 \pm 0.028}$.  At the equilibration time, the KZM further predicts that the average vortex density scales as $\langle n \rangle \propto \tau_Q^{-1/2}$, in agreement with the fit shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling} (c), where $\langle n \rangle = \left( 225.140 \pm 19.016 \right) \tau_Q^{-0.502 \pm 0.025}$. 


{\it Compressible and incompressible energy spectra.}
By introducing the density-weighted velocity field $\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{r})=|\Psi_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathbf{r})|\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{r})$ \cite{Nore1997PRL,Nore97,Ogawa2002,Kobayashi2005}, the kinetic energy of the newborn BEC takes the form 
\begin{equation}
E=\frac{m}{2}\int |\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{r})|^2 \, d^2 \mathbf{r} . 
\end{equation}
Using the canonical decomposition \cite{Nore1997PRL,Nore97,Ogawa2002,Kobayashi2005}, $\mathbf{u}$ can be separated into a compressible (irrotational) component $\mathbf{u}_c$, satisfying $\nabla \times \mathbf{u}_c = 0$, and an incompressible (solenoidal) component $\mathbf{u}_{i}$, for which $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}_{i} = 0$. The total kinetic energy is accordingly decomposed into its compressible and incompressible contributions,
\begin{equation}
E_{i,c} = \frac{m}{2} \int |\mathbf{u}_{i,c}(\mathbf{r})|^2 \, d^2 \mathbf{r}.
\label{eq:energy_cic}
\end{equation}
It is particularly useful to express Eq.~(\ref{eq:energy_cic}) in Fourier space, resulting in
$E_{i,c} = \int E_{i,c}(k) \, d k$, with
\begin{equation}
E_{i,c}(k) \coloneqq
\frac{m}{2}
k
\int 
|\boldsymbol{\tilde{u}}_{i, c} ( \boldsymbol{k} )|^{2} \, d \varphi_k,
\label{eq:energy_cic_fft}
\end{equation}%
where $\mathbf{k} = k ( \cos \varphi_k, \sin \varphi_k )$ and $\boldsymbol{\tilde{u}}_{i,c}(\mathbf{k})$ the Fourier transform of $\mathbf{u}_{i,c}(\mathbf{r})$ \cite{Bradley12}.

{\it Kolmogorov scaling of the energy spectrum in SQT.} %---
The occurrence of SQT in the newborn BEC is diagnosed by the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum 
$E_i \left( k \right)$ \cite{Tsubota13,Neely13}, which has been experimentally measured in 2D BECs \cite{Zhao25}. 
The Kolmogorov scaling is originally derived for homogeneous isotropic turbulence where large eddies (with high Reynolds number) are continuously broken into smaller eddies (with small Reynolds number), and there exists a range of length scales (inertial range) where the details of energy injection and dissipation can be neglected and the eddies follow universal statistical properties \cite{Frisch1995,Barenghi2023textbook}. 
%\textcolor{blue}{
%One may expect to see the inertial range in SQT, since vortices created via the KZM have been shown to follow a universal spatial statistics captured by a Poisson point process with density fixed by the KZM \cite{Thudiyangal24,Massaro2025}. 
%}
With the additional assumptions that, in the inertial range, 
the change of the kinetic energy per unit time 
is independent of $k$ and the viscosity does not affect $E_i(k)$, 
dimensional arguments imply a $k^{-5/3}$ scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum in the inertial range, independent of spatial dimensionality \cite{Vinen02,Barenghi2023textbook}. 
This Kolmogorov scaling 
is a direct reflection of the velocity-velocity autocorrelation function \cite{Kolmogorov41,Frisch1995}. 
At higher momenta, %this 
the 
inertial range
over which the Kolmogorov scaling extends is interrupted by a second power law, $E_i(k) \propto k^{-3}$, that reflects the short-range properties of the vortex core and is thus unrelated to turbulence \cite{Bradley12,Tanogami21}. 
As the radius of the vortex core is of the order of the condensate healing length $\xi_h := \sqrt{\hbar^2 / 2 m \mu_f}$ \cite{ginzburg1958,pitaevskii2003bose,Tanogami21}, the inertial range lies below $k < 2 \pi / \xi_h$. 
For moderated quenches in which the KZM describes the dependence of the vortex density on the quench time, 
the Kolmogorov scaling is verified as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (a) and (b). 
%
\begin{figure}[ptb]
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{QT_E_i_E_c_vs_k.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Incompressible and compressible kinetic energy spectra}.
Panels (a) and (b) show the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum $E_{i}(k)$ at the equilibration time following quenches with $\tau_{Q}=20$ and $\tau_{Q}=100$, respectively, each averaged over $\mathcal{R}=1000$ independent noise realizations. The corresponding fitted power-law exponents are $\alpha = 1.689 \pm 0.020$, $\beta = 3.037 \pm 0.015$ for (a), and $\alpha = 1.662 \pm 0.022$, $\beta = 3.035 \pm 0.015$ for (b). Panels (c) and (d) display the compressible spectrum $E_{c}(k)$ for the same quench protocols, with fit parameters $(\eta, \beta)=(0.986 \pm 0.031, 3.048 \pm 0.015)$ and $(1.023 \pm 0.013, 3.079 \pm 0.015)$ respectively.
The vertical dotted lines mark $k = 2\pi / d_v$, where $d_v = 4 \xi_h$ gives a good estimate of the vortex diameter in our system. 
Shaded error bands correspond to one standard deviation.}
\label{fig:E_vs_k}
\end{figure}
%
The fitting lines in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (a) are $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 934.921 \pm 19.970 \right) k^{-1.689 \pm 0.020}$ and $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 7321.965 \pm 460.356 \right) k^{-3.037 \pm 0.015}$. 
Those in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (b) are $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 166.356 \pm 2.886 \right) k^{-1.662 \pm 0.022}$ and $E_i \left( k \right) = \left( 1079.033 \pm 68.013 \right) k^{-3.035 \pm 0.015}$. The power-law fitted exponents in the inertial range are thus in agreement with the Kolmogorov scaling value $5/3\simeq 1.67$ within the numerical uncertainty. 
While the KZM accurately describes the universal critical dynamics across the BEC transition, it does not account for other effects such as the annihilation of vortex-antivortex pairs, the losses of vortices at the edges of the trap, and coarsening. 
%For slow quenches, defect losses are pronounced, and 
%\textcolor{blue}{
%the inertial range decreases. 
%}
For slow quenches, the system evolves nearly adiabatically, resulting in suppressed vortex formation and diminished turbulence. As a consequence, the inertial range shrinks and the Kolmogorov scaling becomes less pronounced.  

%the KZM no longer describes the scaling of the vortex density as a function of the quench time. 
%Similarly, the scaling of $E(k)$ deviates from the Kolmogorov prediction as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (b), where best fits are $E \left( k \right) \sim \left( 107.206 \pm 3.085 \right) k^{-5/3}$ and $E \left( k \right) \sim \left( 483.247 \pm 0.841 \right) k^{-3}$ for $\tau_Q = 200$.

Although our main interest lies in the Kolmogorov scaling of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum, we also plotted $E_c(k)$ at the equilibration time, as a function of $k$ in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (c) and (d). 
For the 2D system at equilibrium, it is expected that $E_c(k) \propto k$ for small $k$ \cite{Numasato2010}, which can also be explained from the phonon emission in  vortex-antivortex annihilation \cite{Ogawa2002,Kozik2005,Kwon2021,Shinn2025} occurring at the length scale 
$\sim 4 \xi_h$, as the vortex core radius can be estimated to be $2 \xi_h$ from our numerical data, in agreement with Refs. \cite{ginzburg1958,pitaevskii2003bose}. 
In addition, since $E(k)$ follow the $k^{-3}$ law at large $k$ \cite{Nore97}, $E_c(k)$ is expected to follow the same power law. Our numerical results in Fig. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (c) and (d) are in agreement with these expectations. 
Note that $E_c(k)$ and $E_i(k)$ are in the same order in Figs. \ref{fig:E_vs_k} (b) and (d) for $\tau_Q = 100$, showing that one should analyze $E_i(k)$ to see the Kolmogorov scaling, rather than $E(k)$. Similar results with $E_c(k)$ being comparable to $E_i(k)$ are also reported, i.e., in \cite{Numasato2010}. 








\begin{figure}[ptb]
\includegraphics[width=0.9\columnwidth]{KZ_scaling_E_i_Kolmogorov.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Kibble-Zurek universality of the incompressible kinetic energy spectrum in the inertial range}. 
Panel (a) shows $E_i \left( k \right)$ at equilibration time for various $\tau_{Q}$ values, each averaged over $\mathcal{R}=1000$ stochastic realizations. 
The dotted vertical line marks 
$k = 2\pi / d_v$ with the vortex diameter estimated to be $d_v = 4 \xi_h$. 
Panel (b) displays $E_i \left( k \right) \tau_Q^{1/2}$ as a function of the scaled momentum $k \tau_Q^{1/4} \propto k \hat{\xi}$. 
In both panels, shaded error bands indicate one standard deviation.
}
\label{fig:Ei_vs_k_KZM}
\end{figure}

Having established that the Kolmogorov scaling describes thermal quenches for two different values of $\tau_Q$, we establish the nonequilibrium universality dictated by KZM in SQT. To this end, we report $E_i \left( k \right)$ in Fig. \ref{fig:Ei_vs_k_KZM} for various quench times.
According to KZM, the kinetic energy associated with localization over a length scale set by the KZ correlation length $\hat{\xi}$ and the corresponding wavector are given by 
\begin{equation}
\hat{E}=\frac{\hbar^2\hat{k}^2}{2m}=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m\xi_0^2}\left(\frac{\tau_0}{\tau_Q}\right)^{\frac{2\nu}{1+z\nu}},\quad \hat{k}=\frac{1}{\hat{\xi}}=\frac{1}{\xi_0}\left(\frac{\tau_0}{\tau_Q}\right)^{\frac{\nu}{1+z\nu}}. 
\end{equation}
Thus, it is natural to rescale $E_i(k)$ and $k$ by $\hat{E}$ and $\hat{k}$, respectively. Fig. \ref{fig:Ei_vs_k_KZM} (b) shows that curves of $E_i(k)$ for different $\tau_Q$ admit a universal collapse when using the KZ variables, i.e., plotting $E_i(k)\hat{\xi}^2$ as a function of  $k\hat{\xi}$ in terms of the KZ correlation length $\hat{\xi} \propto\tau_Q^ {1/4}$. This is in contrast to $E_c(k)$, which does not admit universal collapse, as discussed in \cite{SM}. 
In addition, for $k$ exceeding $d_v^{-1}$, the lack of collapse for $E_i(k)$ when rescaled by the KZ variables in the $k^{-3}$ scaling regime is expected as it is governed by an equilibrium quantity, the vortex core radius, that is independent of the quench rate. 

Deviations from the universal collapse can also be expected for fast quenches (e.g., $\tau_Q<10$), for which the KZM breaks down \cite{Zeng23,Xia24} due to the saturation of the defect density shown in \ref{fig:density_and_phase_psi_with_marked_vortices_and_KZ_scaling}(c). However,  such deviations may be taken into account by using the associated correlation length and energy scale, which scale universally with the quench depth rather than the quench time.
Naturally, other non-universal processes beyond KZM and its extensions, governing the evolution after the formation of the BEC, such as atom losses, vortex-antivortex annihilation and coarsening, are expected to suppress the Kolmogorov scaling and SQT. 

{\it Discussion and conclusion.} %---
In summary, we have shown that superfluid formation triggered by a finite-time  quench gives rise to SQT. To this end, we have established that the incompressible kinetic energy of the emergent nonequilibrium BEC exhibits Kolmogorov scaling with respect to the wavevector. Moreover, using the nonequilibrium correlation length predicted by KZM and the associated energy scale, we have shown that the Kolmogorov scaling admits a universal collapse  
for different values of the quench time.
Our findings highlight the rich interplay between distinct yet complementary notions of universality: the universal scaling of energy spectra in turbulent flows and the universal defect dynamics across continuous phase transitions.

{\it Note.} %---
During the completion of this work, Ref. \cite{Yang2025} reported the study of a 4D superfluid with Kolmogorov scaling for the total energy, rather than the incompressible energy in the 2D case we discuss. 
%In general, the incompressible energy follows the Kolmogorov scaling, whereas the compressible energy may not do so (e.g., \cite{Kobayashi2005,Tsubota13,Barenghi2023textbook}), leading to the conclusion that the total energy may not follow the Kolmogorov scaling depending on the system. Therefore, to study the universality of the turbulence, one should look for the incompressible energy as we studied. 


